**Verdict: REJECT** (as a plan ready to implement). Four findings are architectural defects in the proposed pipeline, not deferred work. The rest are implementation gaps or completion-criteria omissions that a revision can absorb. Plan file below is abbreviated as `plan.md`; the design doc as `cat.md` (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).

## Findings

| Severity | file:line | Invariant / evidence | Recommended correction |
|---|---|---|---|
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:14-15, 50-52 | Two payload kinds share one medium (S2) and every consumer must discriminate by bare shape. `dao.space.index` admits a batch only if every element is a tx record or a d5 row (`index.cljc:814-820`, `:827-846`) and rejects the **whole batch** otherwise. An `:application` row `[addr :application op operands tail?]` is 5 wide with a keyword second, and address form is unspecified, so rows either poison a datom batch or, if addresses ever become integers, are silently ingested as datoms. `datom.world.md:34`: source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a slot. `cat.md:1776-1784` marks the batch shape unverified. | Separate media for program rows and ledger datoms, or one declared envelope per batch (`{:yin/batch …}` of cat.md §8.5 vs `{:dao.space/transaction …}`). Never discriminate on tuple width. |
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:50-51, 55 | Compiler emits bare rows and drops the ordered batch `{:yin/batch :yin/root :yin/declarations :yin/harvest}` (cat.md:1559-1563, §9.1 frontends row :1749-1751). Rows carry no root fact (cat.md:368-370), so an evaluator observing S2 cannot know which row to run; macro declarations and the harvest catalogue vanish (blocker 4, cat.md:1850-1863). | The compiler's unit of emission is one §8.5 batch value per admitted source batch. Evaluators load `(nth batch root)`. |
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:39, 52 | Side table `[:db/add occ-e :yin.code/variable-name "my-var"]` has no occurrence key. Design rule: variable names are `[origin root-address path i name]`, keyed by `[origin root path]` never by address alone (cat.md:402-413, :423); `origin` needs `[:source medium batch j]` whose medium identity is not fixed (blocker 3, :1834-1843). The "preserved bijectively" claim on plan.md:39 is unverifiable without the key. Re-running the compiler after a failed `load` re-reads the batch (`observer.cljc:236-240`) and mints fresh tempids, duplicating facts unless identity attrs are `:db/unique` (`transact.cljc:302-319`), which the plan never declares. | Define the occurrence entity: `:yin.occ/origin`, `:yin.occ/root`, `:yin.occ/path` as unique identity, with `medium` and `batch` supplied by the composition (the source-text stream's batch coordinate is a natural source). State idempotency of re-emission. |
| **HIGH (defect)** | plan.md:17-18, 61 | `dao.space.index/transact!` does not exist (`index.cljc:736` is `session`; the transactor is `dao.space.transactor/transact!` at `transactor.cljc:231`). The plan conflates a writer (transactor, single-writer T6) with an observer. Under `dao.space.index.as-observer.md` decisions 1, 4, 5 the index observes any medium directly, never allocates `t`, and resolves tempids itself in `:unresolved` mode (`index.cljc:596-627`, `:905-925`). A "Space Transactor" that re-transacts observed datoms restamps `t` and double-writes the ledger. | Delete the Space Transactor stage. Run an `index/session` in `:unresolved` mode over the ledger medium; the compiler appends bare tempid datoms. |
| **MEDIUM (gap)** | plan.md:39, 59-60 | The §4.2 encoder block (cat.md:574-587, §10.1) is never mentioned. "Same content hash" for alpha-equivalent lambdas and an `$ast` relation that collapses rows on id are identity uses that are blocked until `dao.jing`'s encoding is fixed; `cat.md:791-795` says a stored set may not be collapsed on id equality yet. | Either gate the indexer's dedup and any equal-hash test behind the encoder fix, or state that the indexer carries addresses without collapsing and test that. |
| **MEDIUM (contradiction)** | plan.md:23-33 | The "Semantic VM" observes AST rows directly while a separate "Register VM Compiler" emits "Register Bytecode". In the design the semantic VM executes the instruction vector, and the AST-medium path is `lower` composed in front (`cat.md:905-921`, `linearize.cljc:269-286`). No design doc defines a Register VM or Native Compiler. The lowering must emit a `:derive` ledger record with the `"ast-v1"` profile (cat.md:670-677) and the instruction-provenance side table (§5.3), and the profile plus the `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4/§5.3 amendment must be published first (blockers 7, 14). The plan emits none of these. | Rename stages to the design's terms: rows → lower under profile → instruction vector. Have the lowering observer emit derivation-record datoms. Move Register VM and Native Compiler out of scope explicitly. |
| **MEDIUM (contradiction)** | plan.md:43 vs :48 | Line 43 assigns De Bruijn resolution to the frontend; line 48 assigns it to `ast_walker.cljc` and `linearize.cljc`. `ast_walker.cljc` is an evaluator and emits nothing. Design: resolution happens in the frontend boundary projection (cat.md:360, §9.1 frontends row); linearize only maps `:variable index` to `:var index`; the walker resolves indices against a positional env (cat.md:1731). | Move index computation into `yang.*` projection. Limit linearize and walker changes to consuming indices. |
| **MEDIUM (gap)** | plan.md:47, cat.md:1785-1793 | `engine/executable-program-datom?` (`engine.cljc:511-513`) tests the attribute namespace of a d5 row. A row `[addr :application …]` destructured as `[e a v t m]` has `a` = `:application`, namespace nil, so rows are silently filtered out as non-program input. The plan does not address this, though the design lists it as a required integration item. | Define the row-batch input predicate on the batch envelope, not on element shape. |
